---
title: 'How to Find and Contact Candidates Who Want to Be Found: Consent-Based Outreach'
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** GetHired matches each of your jobs against candidates who have made their profiles public, ranks them with a match score and a reason, and lets you message them from the platform. The candidate replies Interested or Not interested in their dashboard. Their contact details and resume file are shared with you only after they say yes or apply. Because every candidate chose to be found, they're far more likely to answer than a cold email.

Sourcing is where most startup hiring goes wrong. You find a promising profile, guess their email, send a careful note and never hear back. The candidate may have been perfect, but you were one of fifty recruiters in their inbox that week.

GetHired takes a different approach: candidates opt in to being discovered, and every conversation starts on the platform.

## Step 1: Let the matches come to you

When you publish a job, GetHired finds candidates in its talent pool whose skills, experience and preferences fit. Matching works on meaning, not exact keywords, followed by an AI re-ranking step. Our older post on [vector search for candidate screening](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/beyond-keywords-how-vector-search-and-ai-are-revolutionizing-candidate-screening) explains the idea.

Open **Profiles** with a job selected and each candidate card shows:

- a **match score** from 0 to 100;
- a **one-line summary** of why they match.

Open a profile for the **Why this candidate** card:

- **Strengths:** what lines up with the job.
- **Gaps:** what's missing or unclear.
- **Conflicts:** preference mismatches, such as "wants remote; this job is onsite in Pune" or a salary expectation above your range.

Explanations for your top matches are generated in the background. For anyone else, click "Why this match?" and it's generated on demand.

You'll also get a weekly email listing new matches for each active job, with the top three and their summaries.

## Step 2: Save and shortlist

Save candidates you like into named **talent lists**, such as "Backend shortlist" or "Future design hires". You can keep notes and ratings on talent-pool candidates too, so your team remembers why someone stood out.

## Step 3: Send an outreach

From a candidate's card, their profile, a talent list or the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat), click **Contact** and write:

- a subject;
- a short message;
- optionally, a job to invite them to apply for.

Not sure what to write? The **AI draft** button writes a short message based on why this person matched your role. Edit it so it sounds like you. You can also start from one of your email templates.

### What makes a good outreach message

1. **Lead with the match.** "Your work on the payments API at a fintech startup is close to what we're building" beats "I came across your profile".
2. **Say what the role is** in one sentence, with the salary range if you can.
3. **Make the ask small.** "Open to a 20-minute chat?" gets more yeses than "Please apply".
4. **Keep it under 120 words.**

## Step 4: The candidate answers on GetHired

The candidate gets an email with your message and a link to their **Opportunities** page. There they see your company, the job and why they were matched, and they can answer:

- **Interested**, with an optional note and a preferred way to reach them;
- **Not interested**, with an optional reason such as not looking, location or compensation.

When they say Interested, you get an email and a badge in the app, and their contact details and resume file become visible to your company. If you attached a job, they can apply in one click, and the application is marked as coming from outreach.

## Step 5: Track replies

The **Outreach** page lists every message your company sent, filterable by job and status, with your reply rate. Each candidate's profile shows the status of your outreach to them.

## Why contact details stay hidden

This is the core of how GetHired works, so it's worth explaining.

For talent-pool candidates who haven't applied or said yes, you see their directory profile: desired roles, skills, experience, preferred locations, salary range, work style, career goals and their resume sections with contact details removed. You don't see their email, phone, LinkedIn, GitHub or original resume file.

This is enforced in the database, not just hidden on the screen. It means:

- **Candidates trust the platform**, so more of them keep their profiles public.
- **Your message isn't buried** under scraped cold emails.
- **Replies are real.** Someone who answers Interested wants to talk.

Candidates can also turn off outreach entirely or block a company.

## Fair limits that protect everyone

- An outreach expires after **30 days** without an answer.
- You can't send a second message for the same candidate and job while one is open.
- One company can contact the same candidate at most **3 times in 90 days**.
- Each plan includes a monthly outreach allowance. See [pricing](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/pricing/companies).

## Bonus: signals candidates choose to share

Applicants can attach their GetHired AI mock interview or resume review to an application. You'll see a company-perspective summary of the mock interview (competency scores, evidence and suggested follow-up questions), clearly labelled as candidate-shared. The transcript itself is never shared.

[Create a company account](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true) and see who already matches your next role.
